            <ArticleTitle>Microscopic polyangiitis initiated with liver dysfunction, calf pain and fever of unknown origin.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1651-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00296-009-1121-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report herein a case of microscopic polyangiitis (MPA), presenting onset with a spiking fever, liver/biliary dysfunction without jaundice and calf pain without elevation of serum creatine phosphokinase. During 1 month of careful examinations for initial diagnosis, the patient developed renal dysfunction and pulmonary hemorrhage. Based on the results of positive MPO-ANCA, renal and pulmonary involvements, the patient was diagnosed with MPA and treated with high-dose prednisolone and oral cyclophosphamide. Soon after initiation of the treatment, symptoms such as fever, calf pain, liver/biliary dysfunction and renal dysfunction disappeared with decrease of MPO-ANCA titer to the normal level.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
